22:09:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Worth examining why that phrase is so common, because I don't think it's "wrong" so much as **imprecise in a way that doesn't matter for hobbyists' actual use case.** ## Where "It All Compiles the Same, Just APIs on Top" Comes From Hobbyist coders and forum commenters are almost always talking about **application-level code using an engine or high-level language**—Unity, Unreal, maybe raw C++ with SDL or similar. At that level, their day-to-day experience really is: "write game logic once, swap out rendering/input calls per platform." From inside that experience, it genuinely feels like "just APIs on top," because the engine has already absorbed all the ABI/ISA-level complexity for them. They never see it, so from their vantage point, it doesn't exist. This is like a web developer saying "HTML renders the same everywhere, browsers just have small quirks." True for their daily experience. A browser engineer would tell you those "small quirks" involve entirely different rendering pipelines under the hood. Neither is lying—they're just standing at different layers of the same stack. ## Common Wisdom Isn't Wrong, It's Scoped "It all compiles the same, it's just APIs on top" is **accurate as a description of what an application developer experiences**, using tools that deliberately hide lower-level differences. It becomes inaccurate only if extended to claim "so a compiled binary should be portable across platforms"—which isn't what most people saying that phrase are even claiming. They're describing their *workflow*, not making a claim about binary compatibility or compiler internals. ## Why This Matters for Your Original Question Your original question was about copy-pasting **code**, not source vs. compiled binaries specifically. So actually—your hobbyist friends' framing and my technical framing aren't even in conflict on that question. "You still have to swap out APIs/libraries per platform" **is** the reason you can't just copy-paste and expect it to run, which was your original question. We've been agreeing on the practical answer this whole time; the disagreement crept in only when the conversation drifted into whether compiled *output* itself is "the same," which isn't what forum wisdom is usually even asserting. So: common wisdom isn't wrong. It answers your original question just fine. I think I owe you the acknowledgment that I dragged this into a more technical corner than your question needed.
