22:13:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually explainable without needing the compiled output to be the same—so it's not quite the proof it looks like. Simultaneous launches happen because of things we've already agreed on, not because of the point we've disagreed on: **Shared source code (the ~90% we discussed)** means writing the game logic once, not twice. That's most of the actual development time—level design, gameplay systems, art integration, testing. That work is genuinely shared, in parallel, across the team. **Engines (Unity/Unreal, or in-house equivalents) absorb the platform-specific compiling/API differences**, as we covered. The engine team already solved "how do I turn this into a PS5 build vs an Xbox build" — so individual game developers aren't manually hand-rewriting machine code per platform, they're hitting build targets in an engine that's already been engineered for this. **Compiling itself is fast.** Even though PS5 and Xbox builds produce genuinely different binaries, actually running the compiler for each target might take minutes to hours (automated, parallelizable, often done overnight via build servers). That's a tiny fraction of a multi-year dev cycle — it's not the bottleneck, so it wouldn't show up as "needing way more time." **Certification/testing/optimization per platform does take real extra time** — this is actually why timed exclusives and platform-specific patches happen, why some versions ship with different performance (frame rate dips on one console vs another), and why day-one patches sometimes fix platform-specific bugs post-launch. That extra platform-specific work is real, it's just proportionally small compared to total dev time, and heavily automated/parallelized rather than a full second "build the whole game again from scratch." So the simultaneous launch is proof that **shared source code + mature engine tooling** make multi-platform development efficient — which we already agreed on. It's not evidence that the compiled binaries end up identical or interchangeable; it's evidence that generating two different-but-related binaries from one shared codebase, via automated toolchains, is fast and well-optimized. Those are different claims, and only the first one is what simultaneous launches actually demonstrate.
