22:16:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Let's separate two things you might be describing, because they're actually different, and I think conflating them is what's keeping this feeling unresolved. **Emulation (PS2-on-PC) really is "translation bolted on afterward."** You're right about that one. The PS2 code was compiled once, for PS2. Nobody changes that code. PCSX2 sits between that unchanged code and your PC, translating at runtime. That's a real example of exactly the pattern you're describing—so if this were the whole conversation, you'd be right. **But native cross-platform releases (PS5 and Xbox versions of the same game) don't work this way at all—there's no translation step, bolted on or otherwise.** Nothing runs through a translator at runtime. The developer's source code gets compiled **twice**, once by a PS5-targeting compiler, once by an Xbox-targeting compiler, and each produces native machine code that the respective console runs directly, with zero translation overhead, at full speed. There's no "after" step here—the difference is baked in at compile time, before the game ever ships. So paradoxically: emulation is the case where your framing is accurate, and it's also the case where performance suffers and things break, precisely because a translator is being bolted on after the fact to paper over a real difference. Native PS5/Xbox builds are the case with *no* translation layer at all, because the compiler just directly generates two different, fully native outputs from the start. So "translation bolted on afterward" doesn't describe compiling-for-different-platforms—it describes a workaround for when that didn't happen (i.e., when someone's running code never compiled for their machine at all). The existence of that workaround is actually more evidence the outputs are different, not less—if they weren't, you wouldn't need a translator for one specific mismatched case while needing none at all for the native side-by-side releases.
